      "quote": "Initializers: Starting a Network in the Right Regime Before the first gradient step, every weight matrix needs a starting distribution. That choice decides whether activations and gradients stay in a usable range through depth, or collapse or explode before learning starts.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-3",
      "quote": "Why initialization matters",
      "kind": "heading",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-4",
      "quote": "A linear layer mixes its inputs: each output is a weighted sum over the fan-in. If the weights are too large, the variance grows layer after layer; if they are too small, it shrinks toward zero. With a nonlinearity in between, the same argument applies to the derivative that carries the gradient backward. The goal is a **critical regime**: variance roughly preserved from layer to layer.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-5",
      "quote": "Initialization also breaks symmetry. If every weight were zero, every hidden unit in a layer would receive the same gradient and stay identical. Random values make units start different. The question is not “random or not”, but **which variance and which correlations** the random values should have.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-6",
      "quote": "Initializer",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-7",
      "quote": "LeCun normal Xavier / Glorot uniform He normal Orthogonal GPT-2 normal (σ = 0.02) Uniform ±1/√fan_in",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-8",
      "quote": "Activation",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-9",
      "quote": "tanh ReLU GELU",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-10",
      "quote": "Fan-in: 256",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-11",
      "quote": "Depth: 24",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why initialization matters"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-12",
      "quote": "The historical ladder",
      "kind": "heading",
      "section": "The historical ladder"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-13",
      "quote": "What to choose today",
      "kind": "heading",
      "section": "What to choose today"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-14",
      "quote": "For a small pre-LN Transformer, **Xavier** is a safe default for tanh-like activations, **He** for ReLU/GELU, and **orthogonal** when you care about stable singular values. GPT-2's $\\sigma=0.02$ with scaled residual projections is a useful reminder that initialization and residual scaling are a package: changing one without the other can push the stream off balance. Framework defaults (for example PyTorch's Kaiming-uniform Linear initialization) are reasonable starting points, not laws; the validation loss is the referee.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "What to choose today"
    },
    {
      "id": "initializers-block-15",
      "quote": "The page above is a variance model, not a substitute for training. It shows the mechanism: change the activation or the fan-in and watch the variance trajectory bend.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "What to choose today"
    }
  ],
